Abstract = {Media coverage of elections in Europe and North America has increasingly
   focused on the campaign as a game rather than a policy debate. This is
   often explained by the changes in media pressures. It may also reflect
   the narrowing of policy space between left and right and the comparative
   prosperity enjoyed in Europe and North America. But the relevance of
   policy varies. The global economic crisis might have led to an increased
   interest in policy among voters and focus on it by media. Ireland
   experienced both extremes of boom and crisis between the late 1990s and
   2011. The Irish case allows us to test the impact of the crisis on media
   framing of elections. This article uses original data from the three
   most recent national elections in Ireland, with a research design that
   holds other pertinent variables constant. We find empirical support for
   the theoretical expectation that the context of the election affects the
   relative focus on campaign or horserace vs. substantive policy issues.},
